Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "constitutional-legal-order-united-states":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"Constitutional and Legal Order of the United States",infoboxKicker:"Federal republic, separated powers, judicial review, and forty-eight states",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-co1a2 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The United States remains a federal presidential republic governed under the Constitution of 1787 and its inherited amendments.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> After 1941 those institutions absorb neutral-era retrenchment, repeated contingent presidential elections, National Renewal government, Free Association, corporate political power, and a mature five-party system without becoming parliamentary or one-party government.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-co1b3 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Separated branches, bicameral Congress, federal courts, judicial review, the Electoral College, and contingent-election rules are inherited constitutional facts.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Free Association, the failed National Unity proposal, statutory repeal of individual federal income tax, post-1993 elector reforms, and mature five-party bargaining are direct setting canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The continued forty-eight-state Union is accepted working canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Complete amendment numbering and text, Court membership, the lower-court map, congressional rules, state constitutions, detailed civil-rights doctrine, and the post-2000 settlement remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"foundation",title:"A surviving eighteenth-century frame",html:"<p>The Constitution remains supreme federal law. No wartime occupation, reconstruction, or revolutionary convention replaces it after divergence. Political movements seeking national direction must therefore win elections, pass statutes, appoint judges, amend the Constitution, or build private institutions within its forms.</p>"},
      {id:"branches",title:"Separated national powers",html:"<p>The <a href='presidency-united-states.html'>president</a> executes law and directs the national administration. The <a href='congress-united-states.html'>Congress</a> legislates, appropriates, investigates, and checks appointments and treaties. The <a href='federal-judiciary-united-states.html'>federal judiciary</a> decides cases under federal law and reviews constitutionality.</p><p>Each branch depends upon the others for parts of its work without becoming responsible to them in the parliamentary sense.</p>"},
      {id:"federal",title:"Federalism and the forty-eight states",html:"<p>The Union contains forty-eight states, each with its own constitution, legislature, executive, courts, taxation, police power, corporation law, and election administration. Alaska and Hawaii remain territories rather than states.</p><p><a href='federalism-forty-eight-state-union.html'>Federalism</a> is both a limit upon Washington and a mechanism by which nonuniform national change occurs.</p>"},
      {id:"elections",title:"Electoral College and contingent government",html:"<p>The 1956, 1968, and 1996 elections produce House-selected presidents. After states divide electors by district or proportion from 1993, contingent selection becomes a recurrent constitutional second round.</p><p>The arrangement resembles coalition bargaining during formation, but a selected president serves a fixed term and does not require continuing House confidence.</p>"},
      {id:"rights",title:"Rights and Free Association",html:"<p>The Bill of Rights and Reconstruction Amendments remain part of the constitutional order. Their postwar development differs sharply because Earl Warren becomes president rather than Chief Justice and no historical Warren Court forms.</p><p>The Free Association Amendment, adopted around 1972–73, protects a broad private right not to associate. It shields private organizations from compulsory integration while permitting exclusion in employment, housing, education, and commerce that historical federal civil-rights law would restrict.</p>"},
      {id:"renewal",title:"National Renewal inside the Constitution",html:"<p>National Renewal governs through elections, legislation, appointments, contracts, credit, and national programs. Buchanan rejects the 1981 proposal for registered parties, emergency political synchronization, occupational representation, and suspension of foreign-directed organizations.</p><p>His formula—“We were given the Republic to restore, not to replace”—marks the defeat of an overt one-party constitutional turn.</p>"},
      {id:"courts",title:"Judicial doctrine",html:"<p>The <a href='supreme-court-united-states.html'>Supreme Court</a> develops national-sovereignty doctrines during the Kennedy and National Renewal eras and contract constitutionalism during Reagan's presidency. Courts can restrain public corporatism while protecting private arbitration, association, expenditure, and contractual communities.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"The mature five-party republic",html:"<p>By 2000 the president usually identifies a national priority, Congress decides methods and appropriations through issue coalitions, and corporations preserve operational memory across both electoral cycles. Legal sovereignty remains public even where technical dependence makes private systems difficult to replace.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"constitution-united-states-after-1941.html",kicker:"Fundamental law",label:"Constitution after 1941"},{href:"presidency-united-states.html",kicker:"Executive branch",label:"Presidency"},{href:"congress-united-states.html",kicker:"Legislative branch",label:"Congress"},{href:"federal-judiciary-united-states.html",kicker:"Judicial branch",label:"Federal Judiciary"},{href:"federalism-forty-eight-state-union.html",kicker:"Divided sovereignty",label:"Federalism"},{href:"five-party-republic.html",kicker:"Mature political order",label:"Five-Party Republic"}],
    facts:[["Inherited constitution",`Constitution of 1787, as amended${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited form",`Federal presidential republic${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Working states",`48${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Inherited legislature",`Congress · House and Senate${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited executive",`President of the United States${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited highest court",`Supreme Court of the United States${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Direct postwar amendment",`Free Association Amendment${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Inherited parliamentary responsibility",`No${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`]]
  }),

  "constitution-united-states-after-1941":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"Constitution of the United States after 1941",infoboxKicker:"Continuing federal charter through neutrality, mission government, and party fracture",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-ct2a3 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The Constitution continues after 1941 as the fundamental law inherited with the institutions and amendments then in force.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The republic changes through interpretation, state election law, ordinary statute, and Free Association rather than wholesale constitutional replacement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its continued forty-eight-state composition is accepted working canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-ct2b4 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Separated powers, federalism, the Electoral College, contingent-election rules, the Bill of Rights, the Reconstruction Amendments, the Sixteenth Amendment, and fixed presidential government are inherited.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Free Association, the failed National Unity proposal, statutory income-tax repeal, and state elector reforms are direct setting canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The forty-eight-state continuation is accepted working canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The exact text and number of Free Association, every later amendment, term-limit and succession law, District rights, and a consolidated 2000 text remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Constitutional inheritance at divergence",html:"<p>The presidency, bicameral Congress, Article III judiciary, federal system, Electoral College, Bill of Rights, Reconstruction Amendments, direct election of senators, and federal income-tax authority all survive the August 1941 point of divergence.</p>"},
      {id:"neutrality",title:"Neutrality without emergency refounding",html:"<p>The United States avoids direct participation in the general war. It therefore receives neither occupation nor the legitimating effect of a victorious mass mobilization. Byrnes narrows New Deal emergency government after Roosevelt's death through ordinary politics rather than a new constitution.</p>"},
      {id:"elections",title:"Contingent election as a recurring rule",html:"<p>The Twelfth Amendment machinery becomes central when no presidential candidate receives an electoral majority. The House chooses the president by state delegation, while the Senate chooses the vice president under its constitutional procedure.</p><p>Repeated use changes political expectation without amending the fixed-term presidency.</p>"},
      {id:"association",title:"The Free Association Amendment",html:"<p>Adopted around 1972–73, the amendment or equivalent constitutional provision protects a broad right not to associate. Supporters present it as personal liberty and limited government; its practical effect is to block comprehensive federal anti-discrimination law and protect exclusionary private institutions.</p><p>Its exact number and text remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"failed",title:"The failed National Unity proposal",html:"<p>The 1981 National Unity and Constitutional Defense Amendment proposes federal party registration, loyalty conditions, emergency coordination, occupational representation, and power against foreign-directed organizations. Buchanan kills it in committee rather than risk replacing republican competition with permanent emergency order.</p>"},
      {id:"tax",title:"Income tax authority after repeal",html:"<p>The 1993 Paycheck Freedom Act repeals the operative federal individual income tax and wage withholding. The Sixteenth Amendment remains. Congress has ceased exercising the authorized tax rather than removed its constitutional power.</p><p>Because the measure is a statute, Reagan can veto it and Congress can override him; a constitutional amendment would not be presented for presidential signature.</p>"},
      {id:"state",title:"State electoral reform",html:"<p>States increasingly allocate presidential electors by district or proportion after 1993. Their separate laws transform national election outcomes without one federal constitutional amendment or uniform formula.</p>"},
      {id:"2000",title:"Constitutional meaning by 2000",html:"<p>The mature republic is stranger than the text appears: voters choose among several mandates, the House frequently supplies the final president, and private corporations execute public objectives. Yet branch terms, state votes, amendment rules, judicial review, and public legal authority remain recognizably constitutional.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"constitutional-legal-order-united-states.html",kicker:"System overview",label:"Constitutional and Legal Order"},{href:"house-selection-of-president.html",kicker:"Twelfth Amendment practice",label:"House Selection of the President"},{href:"free-association-amendment.html",kicker:"Postwar rights settlement",label:"Free Association Amendment"},{href:"national-unity-amendment.html",kicker:"Failed 1981 proposal",label:"National Unity Amendment"},{href:"federal-income-tax-repeal.html",kicker:"Statutory tax change",label:"Paycheck Freedom Act"},{href:"forty-eight-state-union.html",kicker:"Constitutional geography",label:"Forty-Eight-State Union"}],
    facts:[["Adopted",`17 September 1787${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Effective",`4 March 1789${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Direct post-1941 replacement",`None${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Direct postwar amendment",`Free Association Amendment · c. 1972–73${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Direct failed proposal",`National Unity Amendment · 1981${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Inherited income-tax authority",`Retained in Constitution${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Direct elector-allocation reform",`State law after 1993${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Complete 2000 amendment list","Open"]]
  }),

  "presidency-united-states":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"Presidency of the United States",infoboxKicker:"Fixed-term executive, commander-in-chief, veto, appointment, and national mandate",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-pr3a4 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The presidency is the inherited constitutional executive office of the federal republic, filled through an Electoral College majority or House contingent election and independent of parliamentary confidence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> In the setting it develops from neutral-era restraint into mission-state direction and, by the mature five-party republic, selection of a broad national priority.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-pr3b5 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Executive and commander-in-chief authority, veto and override, appointment with Senate consent, fixed terms, and the contingent-election mechanism are inherited.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The presidential sequence through Hale, House selections in 1956, 1968, and 1996, peaceful 1985 transfer, Hale's bounded mandate, and the mature priority-selector role are direct canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Complete Cabinets, succession acts, term-limit history, Executive Office structure, emergency doctrine, veto totals, the vice-presidential sequence, and the 2000 successor remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"office",title:"Constitutional office",html:"<p>The president heads the executive branch, enforces federal law, appoints officers and judges subject to Senate consent, conducts diplomacy, commands the armed forces, signs or vetoes legislation, and reports upon the state of the Union.</p>"},
      {id:"selection",title:"Election and contingent selection",html:"<p>An Electoral College majority normally selects the president. When no majority exists, the House chooses among the constitutional finalists by state delegation. Earl Warren, Nelson Rockefeller, and Andrew Prescott Hale enter office through this route in 1957, 1969, and 1997.</p>"},
      {id:"fixed",title:"A fixed term",html:"<p>House selection does not create a ministry responsible to a congressional majority. The president retains the executive for the constitutional term unless removed through impeachment, death, resignation, or incapacity procedures.</p>"},
      {id:"mission",title:"The mission-state presidency",html:"<p>Kennedy, Rockefeller, Buchanan, and Reagan use the office to orient national projects in hemispheric security, aerospace, industry, computing, energy, and trade. Congress decides money and law, but presidential attention gives a program national hierarchy and executive coordination.</p>"},
      {id:"veto",title:"Veto and override",html:"<p>The president may return legislation to Congress. A two-thirds vote in each House can override. Reagan's veto of individual income-tax repeal is overridden during the Seventeen Days, while several corporatist measures fail to assemble the same supermajority.</p>"},
      {id:"transfer",title:"The 1985 transfer",html:"<p>Pat Buchanan's certification of Reagan's victory, preservation of records and strategic schedules, and exclusion of security agencies from partisan resistance help repair the legitimacy wound left by the 1968 Stolen Mandate.</p>"},
      {id:"priority",title:"The national priority selector",html:"<p>After 1996 presidents increasingly receive a bounded assignment—recovery, security, household order, decentralization, or space expansion—rather than ownership of a complete party platform. Congress chooses the method and corporations preserve execution capacity.</p>"},
      {id:"sequence",title:"Presidential sequence",html:"<p>Roosevelt is followed by Byrnes, Dewey, Warren, Kennedy, Rockefeller, Buchanan, Reagan, Harwood, and Hale. The <a href='presidents.html'>list of presidents</a> records dates, parties, portraits, and paths to office.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"constitutional-legal-order-united-states.html",kicker:"Constitutional setting",label:"Constitutional and Legal Order"},{href:"presidents.html",kicker:"Officeholders",label:"List of Presidents"},{href:"house-selection-of-president.html",kicker:"Contingent route",label:"House Selection of the President"},{href:"presidential-mandate-culture.html",kicker:"Mature office",label:"Presidential Mandate Culture"},{href:"three-clocks-american-government.html",kicker:"Institutional timing",label:"Three Clocks of Government"},{href:"hale-administration.html",kicker:"Bounded presidency",label:"Hale Administration"}],
    facts:[["Office created",`1789${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited branch",`Executive${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited selection",`Electoral College or House contingent election${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited term character",`Fixed${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited commander-in-chief",`President${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited legislative check",`Veto, subject to congressional override${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Direct mature political function",`Select national priority${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Direct officeholder from 1997",`Andrew Prescott Hale${americanConstitutionCanon}`]]
  }),

  "congress-united-states":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"Congress of the United States",infoboxKicker:"House, Senate, legislation, appropriation, oversight, and constitutional bargaining",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-cg4a5 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Congress is the inherited bicameral federal legislature of House and Senate, with lawmaking, revenue, appropriation, oversight, appointment, treaty, amendment, and impeachment functions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> In the mature five-party republic it also supplies the bargaining arena for contingent presidential selection and repeated changes in how national missions are executed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-cg4b6 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Legislation, appropriation, veto override, impeachment, amendment proposal, and contingent-election powers are inherited.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The 1981 defeat of National Unity, 1993 rupture, and post-1996 issue coalitions and corporate bargaining are direct canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The forty-eight-state basis, retained 435-member House and ninety-six-member Senate, exact party ranges, and much of the mature procedural model are accepted working reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Committee maps, leadership chronology, districts, roll calls, filibuster practice, delegate rights, staff size, and post-2000 organization remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"houses",title:"Two chambers",html:"<p>The <a href='house-representatives-united-states.html'>House</a> represents population through districts and faces election every two years. The <a href='united-states-senate.html'>Senate</a> gives each of forty-eight states two members serving staggered six-year terms.</p>"},
      {id:"law",title:"Legislation and appropriation",html:"<p>A bill normally requires passage in both chambers and presidential signature or a successful override. Congress establishes departments, courts, taxes, spending, criminal law, corporate rules, armed forces, commerce regulation, and national programs within constitutional authority.</p>"},
      {id:"oversight",title:"Oversight and investigation",html:"<p>Committees question officials, demand records, hold hearings, investigate programs, and condition appropriations. Congressional inquiries expose the Rockwell prosecution failure, foreign corporate ownership, procurement favoritism, and administrative capture.</p>"},
      {id:"constitutional",title:"Constitutional functions",html:"<p>Congress proposes amendments by the required supermajority, counts electoral votes, and participates in contingent elections. The House impeaches and the Senate tries; the House chooses a president and the Senate chooses a vice president when the Constitution requires.</p>"},
      {id:"renewal",title:"National Renewal majorities",html:"<p>National Renewal wins strong congressional positions under Buchanan and veto-proof nominal majorities during the Open Door Depression. Factional division prevents those numbers from becoming automatic corporatist legislation.</p>"},
      {id:"seventeen",title:"The Seventeen Days",html:"<p>From 3 to 20 January 1993, the incoming Congress confronts outgoing President Reagan. Several compulsory industrial bills fail to obtain two-thirds unity; the Paycheck Freedom Act overrides his veto and ends the federal individual income tax.</p>"},
      {id:"coalitions",title:"Issue coalitions",html:"<p>After the 1993 party schism, security, space, producer, corporate, family, and regulatory coalitions assemble separately. Supporting a president in contingent selection does not bind a representative or senator to that president's later program.</p>"},
      {id:"capacity",title:"Lawful control and practical dependence",html:"<p>Congress can license, tax, investigate, fund, requisition, prohibit, and reorganize. It cannot quickly reproduce a launch network, industrial supplier system, technical staff, or insurance pool. That gap gives established corporations bargaining power without granting them legislative sovereignty.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"constitutional-legal-order-united-states.html",kicker:"Constitutional setting",label:"Constitutional and Legal Order"},{href:"house-representatives-united-states.html",kicker:"Population chamber",label:"House of Representatives"},{href:"united-states-senate.html",kicker:"States' chamber",label:"United States Senate"},{href:"issue-coalitions-congress.html",kicker:"Mature practice",label:"Issue Coalitions"},{href:"three-clocks-american-government.html",kicker:"Institutional timing",label:"Three Clocks of Government"},{href:"federal-income-tax-repeal.html",kicker:"Override precedent",label:"Paycheck Freedom Act"}],
    facts:[["Inherited type",`Bicameral federal legislature${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Working lower chamber",`House of Representatives · 435 members${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Working upper chamber",`Senate · 96 members${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Working states represented",`48${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Inherited fiscal power",`Taxation and appropriation${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited override threshold",`Two-thirds of each House${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited presidential-selection role",`House by state delegation${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Direct mature coalition form",`Issue-specific${americanConstitutionCanon}`]]
  }),

  "house-representatives-united-states":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"United States House of Representatives",infoboxKicker:"Population chamber, biennial election, impeachment, revenue, and presidential selection",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-hr5a6 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The House of Representatives is the inherited population-based chamber of Congress, elected every two years with revenue, impeachment, appropriation, and state-delegation contingent-election powers.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The alternate working register retains 435 voting seats apportioned among forty-eight states.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-hr5b7 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The two-year cycle, population apportionment, single-member-district inheritance, revenue and impeachment roles, and one-vote-per-state contingent election are inherited.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> House selections in 1956, 1968, and 1996 and the later issue-coalition order are direct canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The retained 435-seat total, exact party ranges, and 1993 vote arithmetic are working reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> District maps, reapportionment statutes, delegate rights, officers, committees, rules, exact 1992 result, and 2000 composition remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"composition",title:"Composition and election",html:"<p>The chamber contains 435 voting representatives apportioned among states by population. Alaska, Hawaii, Puerto Rico, Guam, and other territories do not receive ordinary state voting delegations merely because they lie under federal sovereignty.</p>"},
      {id:"cycle",title:"The two-year clock",html:"<p>Every House seat returns to voters at each biennial federal election. The short cycle allows a national reaction to change appropriations and methods halfway through a presidential term.</p>"},
      {id:"law",title:"Revenue, legislation, and oversight",html:"<p>Revenue bills originate in the House, while both chambers must agree upon legislation and spending. Committees examine departments, corporations, security agencies, ownership, procurement, and the administration of national programs.</p>"},
      {id:"impeachment",title:"Impeachment",html:"<p>The House alone adopts articles of impeachment against a president, judge, or other civil officer. Conviction and removal require a Senate trial rather than a second House vote.</p>"},
      {id:"president",title:"Selection of the president",html:"<p>When no Electoral College majority exists, each state delegation casts one presidential vote among the constitutional finalists. The forty-eight-state Union therefore requires a majority of state delegations, not a majority of individual representatives.</p>"},
      {id:"crises",title:"Three contingent-election eras",html:"<p>Warren's 1956 selection occurs during three-party fracture. Rockefeller's 1968 selection creates the Stolen Mandate. Hale's 1996 selection normalizes the procedure as a negotiated second round.</p>"},
      {id:"majorities",title:"Large majorities and factional limits",html:"<p>National Renewal approaches or exceeds three hundred seats during the depression backlash. A full-attendance two-thirds override requires 290 votes. Nominal party strength still fails when constitutionalists, corporatists, producers, and regional members divide.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"House politics by 2000",html:"<p>Representatives bargain simultaneously by party, state, region, sponsor, and issue. A delegation may help select a president for stability, then oppose that president's regulatory or space method without ending the administration.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"congress-united-states.html",kicker:"Bicameral legislature",label:"Congress"},{href:"united-states-senate.html",kicker:"Other chamber",label:"United States Senate"},{href:"house-selection-of-president.html",kicker:"Contingent function",label:"House Selection of the President"},{href:"issue-coalitions-congress.html",kicker:"Mature practice",label:"Issue Coalitions"},{href:"forty-eight-state-union.html",kicker:"Delegation geography",label:"Forty-Eight-State Union"},{href:"1996-united-states-presidential-election.html",kicker:"Normalized selection",label:"1996 Presidential Election"}],
    facts:[["Inherited chamber",`Lower House of Congress${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Working voting seats",`435${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Inherited term",`Two years${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited apportionment basis",`State population${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited sole constitutional power",`Impeachment${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited revenue role",`Originate in House${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited contingent vote",`One vote per state delegation${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Working two-thirds full House",`290 votes${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`]]
  }),

  "united-states-senate":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"United States Senate",infoboxKicker:"Ninety-six-member chamber of states, appointments, treaties, trials, and staggered continuity",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-sn6a7 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The Senate is the inherited states' chamber, with two directly elected senators per state serving staggered six-year terms and exercising legislative, confirmation, treaty, impeachment-trial, and contingent vice-presidential powers.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The forty-eight-state setting yields a working ninety-six-seat chamber while Alaska and Hawaii remain unrepresented territories.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-sn6b8 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Two senators per state, staggered six-year terms, direct election, legislative equality outside House-origin provisions, advice and consent, impeachment trials, and contingent vice-presidential choice are inherited.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Rejection or dilution of corporatist programs and late issue coalitions are direct canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The continued forty-eight-state Union, territorial Alaska and Hawaii, ninety-six-seat register, party-control ranges, and detailed Reagan-era confirmation pattern remain working reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Membership, classes, leaders, committees, filibuster and cloture rules, treaties, the 1996 vice-presidential result, and 2000 balance remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"composition",title:"Composition",html:"<p>Each state receives two senators regardless of population. The forty-eight-state Union therefore produces ninety-six seats and preserves a chamber in which small interior states possess equal formal weight with California or New York.</p>"},
      {id:"terms",title:"Staggered continuity",html:"<p>Senators serve six-year terms divided into classes, with roughly one-third contested every two years. A presidential landslide or House wave cannot replace the entire chamber at once.</p>"},
      {id:"law",title:"Legislation and appropriation",html:"<p>The Senate must agree to bills and spending passed by the House, and may amend revenue measures after their House origin. Its smaller membership gives individual senators, committees, regions, and factions leverage over national programs.</p>"},
      {id:"consent",title:"Appointments and treaties",html:"<p>Presidential nominations to senior executive office and Article III judgeships require Senate consent. Treaties submitted under Article II require the constitutional supermajority, while executive agreements follow a different political and legal route.</p>"},
      {id:"trials",title:"Impeachment and contingent election",html:"<p>The Senate tries officers impeached by the House. It also chooses the vice president from the constitutional finalists when no candidate receives an electoral majority. The mature record of vice-presidential contingent selection remains less developed than the presidential record.</p>"},
      {id:"renewal",title:"National Renewal and institutional resistance",html:"<p>National Renewal gains control under Buchanan and holds nominally veto-proof strength after the depression backlash. Senate institutionalists and factional disagreements strip compulsory councils and occupational authority from several bills.</p>"},
      {id:"judges",title:"The Reagan confirmations",html:"<p>After Democratic-Republicans strengthen their Senate position in 1986, Reagan probably secures two or three Supreme Court appointments. Their contract constitutionalism helps the judiciary block public corporatism while protecting private association and arbitration.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Senate politics by 2000",html:"<p>Party, state, industry, sponsor, religion, region, and policy cut across one another. The six-year clock makes the Senate a reservoir of earlier coalitions even after the House and presidency change direction.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"congress-united-states.html",kicker:"Bicameral legislature",label:"Congress"},{href:"house-representatives-united-states.html",kicker:"Other chamber",label:"House of Representatives"},{href:"supreme-court-united-states.html",kicker:"Confirmation power",label:"Supreme Court"},{href:"presidency-united-states.html",kicker:"Advice and consent",label:"Presidency"},{href:"three-clocks-american-government.html",kicker:"Six-year clock",label:"Three Clocks of Government"},{href:"forty-eight-state-union.html",kicker:"Representation",label:"Forty-Eight-State Union"}],
    facts:[["Inherited chamber",`Upper House of Congress${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Working seats",`96${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Inherited representation",`Two senators per state${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited term",`Six years, staggered${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited election",`Direct${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited distinct powers",`Appointments · treaties · impeachment trials${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited contingent role",`Select vice president${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Working territorial senators",`None${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`]]
  }),

  "federal-judiciary-united-states":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"Federal Judiciary of the United States",infoboxKicker:"Article III courts, constitutional review, federal law, and life-tenured judges",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-fj7a8 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The federal judiciary is the inherited Article III branch of Supreme Court and congressionally established inferior courts, with jurisdiction over federal constitutional, statutory, treaty, admiralty, and interstate cases.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Its later doctrines are necessarily alternate, but the article's detailed sovereignty, contract, association, corporate, and administrative groupings are a working interpretive map.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-fj7b9 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Article III, the Supreme Court, congressionally created lower courts, presidential nomination, Senate confirmation, good-behavior tenure, judicial review, and separate state courts are inherited.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Judicial enforcement of Free Association and the importance of bankruptcy, arbitration, and corporate public-law conflicts follow direct setting developments.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> National-sovereignty blocs, Reagan contract constitutionalists, and detailed doctrinal alignments are incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Circuits, districts, judges, specialized courts, procedure, caseloads, appointments, and 2000 administration remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"article",title:"Article III foundation",html:"<p>The Constitution creates one Supreme Court and permits Congress to establish inferior federal courts. Judges hold office during good behavior and receive protected compensation, insulating adjudication from ordinary electoral removal.</p>"},
      {id:"structure",title:"Courts established by Congress",html:"<p>District courts hear federal trials, appellate courts review them, and the Supreme Court supplies final federal authority. Congress defines districts, circuits, judgeships, jurisdiction, procedure, and specialized statutory courts subject to constitutional limits.</p><p>The exact alternate 2000 map remains open.</p>"},
      {id:"jurisdiction",title:"Federal jurisdiction",html:"<p>Federal courts hear constitutional and statutory questions, cases involving the United States, interstate disputes, diversity matters, admiralty, bankruptcy, federal crime, regulatory review, and disputes assigned by Congress. State courts retain the larger body of ordinary state law.</p>"},
      {id:"review",title:"Judicial review",html:"<p>Courts may hold legislative or executive action unconstitutional in a proper case. They do not issue general advisory vetoes. Jurisdiction, standing, remedy, precedent, and the facts presented determine how constitutional conflict reaches judgment.</p>"},
      {id:"appointments",title:"Appointments and political formation",html:"<p>Presidents nominate and the Senate confirms. Life tenure does not erase the constitutional vocabulary, professional networks, and governing experience through which a jurist is selected.</p><p>National Renewal influence therefore appears first through sovereignty-minded judges rather than open party uniforms.</p>"},
      {id:"private",title:"Private law and the corporate republic",html:"<p>Bankruptcy courts, contract enforcement, arbitration clauses, property rules, corporate cases, administrative review, and federal remedies become central during the Open Door Depression. Public courts frequently enforce agreements that move underlying disputes into private arbitration.</p>"},
      {id:"rights",title:"Rights and association",html:"<p>Federal courts protect public constitutional citizenship while reading the Free Association Amendment broadly against federal compulsion in private employment, housing, schooling, and association. The result is a rights order that restrains government and legitimates private exclusion.</p>"},
      {id:"2000",title:"Position by 2000",html:"<p>The judiciary can invalidate a compulsory council or unlawful executive act, yet cannot easily replace the corporate systems upon which government depends. Its strongest role is defining which actor may lawfully decide, not operating the machinery after judgment.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"constitutional-legal-order-united-states.html",kicker:"Constitutional setting",label:"Constitutional and Legal Order"},{href:"supreme-court-united-states.html",kicker:"Highest court",label:"Supreme Court"},{href:"united-states-senate.html",kicker:"Confirmation body",label:"United States Senate"},{href:"free-association-amendment.html",kicker:"Rights doctrine",label:"Free Association Amendment"},{href:"corporate-recovery-districts.html",kicker:"Contract administration",label:"Corporate Recovery Districts"},{href:"open-door-depression.html",kicker:"Caseload transformation",label:"Open Door Depression"}],
    facts:[["Inherited constitutional article",`Article III${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited highest court",`Supreme Court${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited inferior courts",`Established by Congress${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited appointment",`President with Senate consent${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited tenure",`During good behavior${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited constitutional power",`Judicial review${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited parallel systems",`State judiciaries${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Complete 2000 court map","Open"]]
  }),

  "supreme-court-united-states":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"Supreme Court of the United States",infoboxKicker:"Highest federal court, judicial review, rights doctrine, and constitutional arbitration",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-sc8a9 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The Supreme Court remains the inherited highest federal court and final national authority on properly presented questions of federal constitutional and statutory law.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Earl Warren instead becomes vice president and president, so the historical Warren Court cannot occur.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The later sovereignty-minded, national-liberal, enterprise-conservative, and contract-constitutional blocs are assistant-developed interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-sc8b1 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->The Court, its inherited nine-seat baseline, and judicial review predate divergence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Warren's absence as Chief Justice and judicial confirmation of broad Free Association are direct canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Approximate 1964 blocs, national-security deference, Reagan's probable two or three appointments, contract constitutionalism, and the Genoa Controls litigation model are incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Chief justices, membership, appointment dates, case names and votes, later seat count, written opinions, and the 2000 Court remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"institution",title:"Constitutional institution",html:"<p>Article III creates the Court while Congress regulates much of its appellate jurisdiction and organization. The historical nine-seat arrangement is the inherited baseline; the archive does not yet supply a complete alternate membership table.</p>"},
      {id:"warren",title:"No Earl Warren Court",html:"<p>Earl Warren becomes Dewey's vice president and then House-selected president in 1957. He never follows the historical route to Chief Justice. The personnel and landmark decisions associated with the historical Warren Court cannot therefore be imported unchanged.</p>"},
      {id:"1964",title:"Approximate blocs by 1964",html:"<p>The Court contains enterprise conservatives, surviving New Deal or labor liberals, Kennedy national liberals, and national-sovereignty jurists. The last group uses constitutional order, foreign penetration, emergency power, and hemispheric security rather than openly partisan National Renewal language.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Security and hemisphere",html:"<p>Doctrines described by scholars as foreign-penetration, hemispheric-security, associated-government-recognition, constitutional-order, and security-compact deference give the executive substantial room in external and subversion cases when Congress later ratifies or funds action.</p><p>Exact controlling cases remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"association",title:"Free Association doctrine",html:"<p>Around 1973 the Court upholds a broad reading of the Free Association Amendment. Public citizenship can be equal while private schools, employers, housing organizations, clubs, and businesses retain extensive constitutional power to refuse association.</p>"},
      {id:"reagan",title:"The Reagan Court",html:"<p>Reagan probably appoints two or three justices after Democratic-Republicans strengthen the Senate in 1986. They emphasize separated powers, private contract and property, limited delegation, foreign-affairs executive power, and protection of voluntary association.</p>"},
      {id:"irony",title:"The contract-constitutional irony",html:"<p>The Court is disposed to invalidate compulsory sector councils, occupational lawmaking, political registration, and privately staffed boards exercising federal taxing power. The same doctrine supports private arbitration, corporate political expenditure, employer services, contractual waivers, and exclusionary residential communities.</p>"},
      {id:"genoa",title:"Genoa Controls and ownership law",html:"<p>The 1986 Genoa Controls case and its quiet legislative or administrative patch encourage false confidence that foreign holdings can be detected investor by investor. Later ownership mapping exposes the weakness of that framework and helps precipitate the 1990 forced divestiture crisis.</p>"},
      {id:"open",title:"Open judicial record",html:"<p>The institution's doctrinal direction is established more firmly than its names. A future justice list must reconcile each vacancy with presidents, Senate control, professional biography, the non-Warren civil-rights path, and the Court's four evolving blocs.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"federal-judiciary-united-states.html",kicker:"Judicial system",label:"Federal Judiciary"},{href:"earl-warren.html",kicker:"Alternate career",label:"Earl Warren"},{href:"free-association-amendment.html",kicker:"Major rights doctrine",label:"Free Association Amendment"},{href:"ronald-reagan.html",kicker:"Appointing president",label:"Ronald Reagan"},{href:"genoa-controls-case.html",kicker:"Ownership precedent",label:"Genoa Controls Case"},{href:"corporate-patronage-united-states.html",kicker:"Private-power setting",label:"Corporate Patronage"}],
    facts:[["Inherited court",`Highest court of the United States${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited constitutional basis",`Article III${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited seat baseline",`Nine${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Direct Chief Justice Earl Warren",`No${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Direct postwar rights doctrine",`Free Association${americanConstitutionCanon}`],["Working Reagan appointments",`Probably two or three${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Working mature judicial tendency",`Contract constitutionalism${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Complete 2000 membership","Open"]]
  }),

  "federalism-forty-eight-state-union":americanConstitutionArticle({
    title:"Federalism in the Forty-Eight-State Union",infoboxKicker:"Divided sovereignty, state police power, election law, territories, and national supremacy",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-fm9a1 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Federalism is the inherited constitutional division between the United States and its member states, preserving state constitutions, courts, domestic government, election law, equal Senate representation, and federal enumerated and implied powers.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The accepted working setting leaves Alaska and Hawaii territorial and the Union at forty-eight states through the mature period.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-fm9b2 writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:55:00-06:00 -->Separate state governments and courts, election administration, general domestic power, equal Senate representation, and federal supremacy within national competence are inherited.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Post-1993 elector allocation, retained state taxation after federal income-tax repeal, and strong federal security and commerce authority are direct canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Forty-eight states, territorial Alaska and Hawaii, the exact 435/96 chamber registers, producer-federalism doctrine, and detailed state corporate-law model are incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> State constitutional changes, governors, districts, local powers, compacts, territorial delegations, and the 2000 balance remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"division",title:"Divided public authority",html:"<p>The federal government possesses constitutional national powers; states retain broad domestic police power and institutional existence. Citizens ordinarily live under federal, state, county, municipal, and sometimes special-district law at once.</p>"},
      {id:"states",title:"Forty-eight states",html:"<p>Arizona's 1912 admission remains the last by 2000. Each state receives two senators and an apportioned House delegation. Alaska and Hawaii remain strategically important territories without equal congressional standing.</p>"},
      {id:"elections",title:"Election administration",html:"<p>States establish much of the machinery for federal elections within constitutional and federal statutory limits. District and proportional elector reforms after 1993 spread through separate state enactments, producing a transformed national system without uniformity.</p>"},
      {id:"corporations",title:"Corporate and association law",html:"<p>Incorporation, contract, property, insurance, professional licensing, and many employment rules remain rooted in state law. The 1998 federal enterprise classification overlays rather than abolishes state corporate forms.</p>"},
      {id:"tax",title:"Taxation after the Paycheck Freedom Act",html:"<p>Federal individual income taxation ends by statute in 1994, but states retain their own income, sales, property, corporate, excise, and other tax arrangements. The national revenue transition therefore does not create one tax-free republic.</p>"},
      {id:"mission",title:"Federal mission and national supremacy",html:"<p>Defense, foreign relations, the hemisphere, interstate transport, strategic industry, currency, national communications, federal procurement, and space programs expand Washington's practical reach. Federal law remains supreme when constitutionally enacted within national competence.</p>"},
      {id:"producer",title:"Producer federalism",html:"<p>Wesley Crane and allied movements defend states, local producers, farms, independent firms, and regional credit against both federal bureaucracies and national corporate systems. Their program uses constitutional federalism as an economic and cultural doctrine.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Unequal federal geography",html:"<p>The state boundary protects representation but does not guarantee equal capacity. Large states dominate population and industry; small states receive equal Senate votes; territories serve national strategy without senators; the District of Columbia remains the federally governed seat whose final political rights are open.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"constitutional-legal-order-united-states.html",kicker:"Constitutional setting",label:"Constitutional and Legal Order"},{href:"forty-eight-state-union.html",kicker:"National geography",label:"Forty-Eight-State Union"},{href:"american-territories-possessions-2000.html",kicker:"Non-state jurisdictions",label:"Territories and Possessions"},{href:"presidential-elector-allocation-after-1993.html",kicker:"State election law",label:"Elector Allocation"},{href:"wesley-crane.html",kicker:"Producer theorist",label:"Wesley Crane"},{href:"american-hemisphere.html",kicker:"Different strategic geography",label:"American Hemisphere"}],
    facts:[["Working member states",`48${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Inherited Senate representation",`Two each${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Working federal Senate",`96 seats${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Working House",`435 voting seats${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Working non-state territories",`Alaska · Hawaii${americanConstitutionExtrapolation}`],["Inherited state power",`General domestic police power${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited federal supremacy",`Within constitutional national competence${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`],["Inherited uniform state constitutions",`No${americanConstitutionPredivergence}`]]
  })
});

